// @emit-error the reassigned function binding `f` is called at more than one argument type
// A REASSIGNED function binding whose calls pin TWO different argument types. One binding
// owns one closure cell, that cell carries one functype, and the two lambdas written into
// it want two — the shape has no lowering, so the emitter says so.
//
// THE LADDER AND THE ARM IT LACKED (#1827, one rung past #1821). `monoMakeInstance` renames
// the callee `Ident` to the instance it resolved, folding a call THROUGH the binding into a
// by-name direct call. Its REGISTRY-HIT rung declines that rename for a capturing lambda
// and — since #1821 — for a REASSIGNED binding. Its APPEND rung (a second distinct SHAPE)
// declined it for the capturing lambda and renamed the reassigned binding anyway, so every
// call at the second shape answered the FIRST lambda. Measured with a counter: this program
// with `n = n + 1` / `n = n + 100` bodies printed 3 where it must print 102 — a silent WRONG
// ANSWER, master's outcome for the generic-FREE twin and the one this reject closes.
//
// The one-argument-type twin is representable and is a `@run` cell in
// `generics/mono-lambda-forwarder-uninstantiated.vl`; annotating the parameters, or binding
// a separate value per type, is what the message asks for.
function bump1<T>(x: T): T { return x }
function bump2<T>(x: T): T { return x }
let f = (a) => bump1(a)
print(f(1))
f = (a) => bump2(a)
print(f(1.5))
